# A DELIBERATE NO-OP. This file exists to disarm formatters, not to run one.
#
# Why a comment was not enough. Visual Studio enables ClangFormat support by
# default and shells out to clang-format.exe as you type whenever a
# `.clang-format` file is present; the VS Code C/C++ extension does the same on
# format-on-save. Neither reads comments. An earlier version of this file said
# "DESCRIPTIVE, NOT ENFORCED" at the top and was still applied by both, so one
# save could reformat three quarters of the tree and bury whatever the
# contributor came to change.
#
# `DisableFormat: true` makes clang-format return its input unchanged. Verified
# 1 Sep 2026 against clang-format 20.1.8 (the copy Visual Studio 18 ships and
# invokes) and 22.1.1: byte-identical output on a probe file that both versions
# reformat without it. Because the IDEs drive the same binary, disabling it here
# disables them. `tools/probe-formatter.ps1` re-runs that proof, and the
# `format-hygiene` invariant fails if this line ever goes missing.
#
# The house style still exists, at `tools/house-style.clang-format`. It is not
# named `.clang-format` precisely so that nothing discovers it, and it is
# applied by hand to a NEW file:
#
#     clang-format --style=file:tools/house-style.clang-format -i <newfile>
#
# What actually governs formatting here: `.editorconfig`, which every editor
# honours and which the `format-hygiene` invariant enforces on every push, and
# `.claude/rules/cpp-conventions.md`, which says to match the lines around the
# ones you touch, by hand.
DisableFormat: true
